{"id": "f4aba31a-4140-4df5-8ac7-a75780c9c8f9", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T11:16:50.486900", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f97b3557-266f-4c9a-a0bf-b3f82bf4984e", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design: Benchmarking Steps-Per-Second Performance of JaxMARL vs. PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread Under Random Actions\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Objective\nThe objective of this experiment is to rigorously quantify and compare the simulation throughput—measured in environment Steps Per Second (SPS) and individual Agent Steps Per Second (ASPS)—of **JaxMARL** (a GPU-accelerated, JAX-native multi-agent reinforcement learning library) against the reference **PettingZoo MPE** implementation (CPU-based Python/NumPy) on the cooperative **MPE Simple Spread** benchmark under a uniform random action policy.\n\nThe experiment evaluates:\n1. Pure execution throughput across environment batch sizes ranging from single-environment execution ($B = 1$) to massively parallel batches ($B = 65,536$).\n2. Scaling characteristics and saturation limits on modern GPU vs. single-core and multi-core CPU baselines.\n3. The empirical speedup factor $S(B) = \\frac{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{JaxMARL}}(B)}{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{PettingZoo}}(B)}$ and identification of the crossover point where GPU batched vectorization outperforms CPU multiprocessing.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Protocol & Benchmarking Methodology\n\n### 2.1. Environment Configuration\n- **Task**: Multi-Particle Environment (MPE) *Simple Spread* (`simple_spread_v3` in PettingZoo; `MPEEnv(env_name=\"simple_spread\")` in JaxMARL).\n- **Agents ($N$)**: 3 cooperative agents.\n- **Landmarks ($M$)**: 3 target landmarks.\n- **Physics & Dynamics**: 2D continuous space with Newtonian kinematic integration, agent-agent collision dynamics, and landmark avoidance/coverage.\n- **Action Space**: Discrete action space with 5 discrete actions per agent:\n  $$\\mathcal{A}_i = \\{\\text{no-op}, \\text{move left}, \\text{move right}, \\text{move down}, \\text{move up}\\}$$\n- **Observation Space**: Continuous vectors containing agent velocity, relative landmark positions, other agent relative positions, and communication signals.\n- **Episode Horizon ($T$)**: Default $T = 25$ steps.\n- **Auto-Reset Policy**: In long-duration rollout benchmarking ($L$ total steps per environment, where $L \\gg T$, e.g., $L = 1,000$), environments automatically reset upon reaching `done` or truncation at $T=25$. Both implementations are configured with identical auto-reset mechanics.\n\n### 2.2. Policy Protocol\n- **Action Selection**: Uniform random sampling over the discrete action space $\\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\}^3$.\n- **Action Generation Isolation**:\n  To measure pure environment simulation speed without skewing results by differences in host vs. device pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) overhead, two benchmarking variants are specified:\n  1. *Isolated Transition Timing (Primary)*: Random action batches are pre-generated prior to the timed loop, isolating the environment `step` computation.\n  2. *End-to-End Simulation Timing (Secondary)*: Action sampling is integrated inside the loop using standard library idioms (`jax.random.categorical` / `jax.random.split` for JaxMARL; NumPy `Generator.integers` for PettingZoo).\n\n### 2.3. Warm-Up & JIT Compilation Protocol\n- **JaxMARL (JAX/GPU)**:\n  - JAX utilizes ahead-of-time/just-in-time compilation via XLA (`jax.jit`). The initial execution triggers compilation graph construction, which must be strictly excluded from throughput measurements.\n  - *Warm-up Phase*: Execute a full warm-up rollout of $L_{\\text{warmup}} = 100$ steps across the target batch size $B$.\n  - Call `jax.block_until_ready()` on the resulting state/observation tree to ensure compilation and initial execution have completely finished on the GPU device before timer initialization.\n- **PettingZoo (CPU)**:\n  - Run a warm-up phase of $L_{\\text{warmup}} = 100$ steps to warm Python bytecode evaluation, dynamic module dispatch, NumPy memory pools, and multi-process IPC pipes.\n\n### 2.4. Device Synchronization & Timing Protocol\n- **Timer**: High-resolution monotonic timer `time.perf_counter_ns()`.\n- **Asynchronous Execution Barrier**: JAX operations are dispatched asynchronously to the GPU. To avoid timing only the host launch overhead, synchronization barriers must bracket the measurement interval:\n  ```python\n  # Synchronize before starting measurement\n  jax.tree_util.tree_map(lambda x: x.block_until_ready(), state)\n  t_start = time.perf_counter()\n\n  # Timed execution loop (e.g., jax.lax.scan or step loop)\n  state, obs, rewards, dones, infos = rollout_fn(state, actions)\n\n  # Synchronize immediately after loop completion\n  jax.tree_util.tree_map(lambda x: x.block_until_ready(), state)\n  t_end = time.perf_counter()\n  ```\n- **Rollout Length ($L$)**: Fixed at $L = 1,000$ consecutive environment steps per parallel worker.\n- **Trial Repetitions**: Each configuration $(B, \\text{framework})$ is repeated over $K = 10$ independent random seeds.\n\n### 2.5. Baseline Execution Architectures\n1. **JaxMARL (GPU)**: Fully vectorized environment step using `jax.vmap` compiled into an optimized monolithic GPU kernel via `jax.jit` / `jax.lax.scan`.\n2. **PettingZoo CPU (Single-Core)**: Sequential execution loop in native Python using PettingZoo Parallel API (`ParallelEnv`).\n3. **PettingZoo CPU (Multi-Core Vectorized)**: Parallelized across CPU cores using Gymnasium's `AsyncVectorEnv` / `SubprocVectorEnv`, deploying $C$ persistent worker processes with shared-memory or inter-process communication (IPC).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Explicit Variables & Parameter Grid\n\n### 3.1. Independent Variables\n| Variable | Notation | Values / Levels |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Framework Implementation** | $F$ | 1. JaxMARL (GPU, CUDA-accelerated)<br>2. PettingZoo MPE (CPU, Single-Core)<br>3. PettingZoo MPE (CPU, Multi-Core $C \\in \\{4, 8, 16, 24\\}$ workers) |\n| **Batch Size (Parallel Envs)** | $B$ | Swept across 17 powers of two:<br>$B \\in \\{2^0, 2^1, 2^2, 2^3, 2^4, 2^5, 2^6, 2^7, 2^8, 2^9, 2^{10}, 2^{11}, 2^{12}, 2^{13}, 2^{14}, 2^{15}, 2^{16}\\}$<br>*(i.e., 1 to 65,536 environments)* |\n| **Action Policy** | $\\pi$ | Fixed: Uniform random action selection |\n| **Random Seeds** | $k$ | 10 independent random seeds ($k \\in \\{1, 2, \\dots, 10\\}$) |\n\n*Note on CPU batch size sweep*: For CPU PettingZoo, $B > 2,048$ may lead to extreme memory and OS process scheduling overhead; CPU sweeps will run from $B = 1$ up to $B = 2,048$ (or host memory ceiling), while GPU sweeps continue through $B = 65,536$.\n\n### 3.2. Dependent Variables & Metrics\n1. **Environment Steps Per Second (SPS)**:\n   $$\\text{SPS} = \\frac{B \\times L}{\\Delta t}$$\n   where $B$ is the number of parallel environments, $L$ is the rollout length ($1,000$ steps), and $\\Delta t = t_{\\text{end}} - t_{\\text{start}}$ is elapsed wall-clock time in seconds.\n2. **Agent Steps Per Second (ASPS)**:\n   $$\\text{ASPS} = N \\times \\text{SPS} = 3 \\times \\text{SPS}$$\n3. **Speedup Factor ($S$)**:\n   $$S_{\\text{single}}(B) = \\frac{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{JaxMARL}}(B)}{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{PettingZoo, 1-core}}(B)}, \\quad S_{\\text{multi}}(B) = \\frac{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{JaxMARL}}(B)}{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{PettingZoo, 24-core}}(B)}$$\n4. **Per-Step Latency ($\\tau$)**:\n   $$\\tau = \\frac{\\Delta t}{L} \\quad (\\text{seconds per batched step})$$\n5. **System Resource Utilization**:\n   - Peak GPU VRAM allocated (GB) and GPU SM compute utilization (%) via NVML.\n   - Peak Host RAM allocated (GB) and aggregate CPU core utilization (%) via `psutil`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls & Confounding Factors\n\n| Potential Confounder | Control Mechanism |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **JIT Compilation Overhead** | Dedicated warm-up run ($L_{\\text{warmup}}=100$) before starting timer; explicit XLA graph caching. |\n| **Asynchronous GPU Dispatch** | Mandatory `.block_until_ready()` synchronization calls placed immediately before start and after end timers. |\n| **I/O & Logging Bottlenecks** | Strict headless execution: disable all graphic rendering, frame saving, telemetry/stdout logging, and TensorBoard/WandB streaming inside benchmark loops. |\n| **Action Generation Discrepancy** | Pre-generate action tensors into memory buffers prior to timed stepping to isolate physics and environment dynamics. |\n| **Python Garbage Collection (GC)** | Explicitly disable GC (`gc.disable()`) during the timed loop interval and invoke manual collection (`gc.collect()`) between trials. |\n| **CPU Frequency Scaling & Turbo Jitter** | Pin CPU governor to `performance` mode; enforce static CPU affinity via `taskset` for worker processes. |\n| **Environment Dynamics Mismatch** | Verify identical physical constants across implementations: landmark radii ($r=0.1$), agent radii ($r=0.15$), acceleration ($a=5.0$), damping friction, collision elastic constants, and observation dimension spaces. |\n| **Memory Allocation Jitter** | Pre-allocate observation and state buffers before the timed loop so no dynamic array resizing occurs during simulation. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Hardware, Software & Environment Specifications\n\n### 5.1. Hardware Infrastructure\n- **Accelerator**: 1x NVIDIA A100-SXM4 (80GB HBM2e memory, PCIe 4.0 / NVLink, compute capability 8.0) or NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24GB G6X).\n- **Host Processor**: AMD EPYC 7763 or Intel Xeon Gold 6338 (24 physical cores / 48 threads enabled, base clock $\\ge 2.45$ GHz).\n- **System Memory**: 128 GB DDR4/DDR5 ECC RAM.\n- **Storage**: Fast NVMe SSD (PCIe Gen4) for environment caching.\n\n### 5.2. Software Stack\n- **Operating System**: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (Kernel 5.15 / 6.5, x86_64).\n- **CUDA Toolchain**: CUDA 12.2, cuDNN 8.9, NVIDIA Driver 535+.\n- **Python Runtime**: Python 3.10.12.\n- **Libraries & Packages**:\n  - `jax`: $\\ge 0.4.23$\n  - `jaxlib`: $\\ge 0.4.23+cuda12.cudnn89`\n  - `jaxmarl`: current stable release\n  - `pettingzoo`: $\\ge 1.24.3$\n  - `gymnasium`: $\\ge 0.29.1$\n  - `numpy`: $\\ge 1.26.0$\n  - `psutil`: $\\ge 5.9.0$\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis and Visualization Plan\n\n### 6.1. Planned Visualizations\n1. **Throughput Scaling Curve (Figure 1)**:\n   - **X-axis**: Number of Parallel Environments $B$ (logarithmic scale, base 2, from 1 to $65,536$).\n   - **Y-axis**: Environment Steps Per Second (SPS) (logarithmic scale, base 10, from $10^2$ to $10^8$).\n   - **Curves**:\n     - JaxMARL (GPU)\n     - PettingZoo (CPU 1-core)\n     - PettingZoo (CPU 8-core)\n     - PettingZoo (CPU 24-core)\n   - Shaded error bands representing 95% confidence intervals across the 10 seeds.\n2. **Speedup Factor vs. Parallelism (Figure 2)**:\n   - **X-axis**: Batch Size $B$ (logarithmic scale).\n   - **Y-axis**: Speedup Ratio $S(B) = \\frac{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{JaxMARL}}}{\\text{SPS}_{\\text{PettingZoo}}}$.\n   - Horizontal reference dashed line at $y = 1.0$ (parity line) marking the crossover threshold.\n3. **Latency Profile (Figure 3)**:\n   - Wall-clock execution time per environment step as a function of $B$, illustrating GPU amortization vs. CPU linear scaling.\n\n### 6.2. Statistical Analysis\n- **Summary Statistics**: Report mean, standard deviation, median, and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals for SPS across all seeds for each condition $(F, B)$.\n- **Empirical Hypothesis Testing**:\n  - Null Hypothesis $H_0$: There is no significant difference between JaxMARL GPU throughput and CPU PettingZoo throughput at batch size $B$.\n  - Alternative Hypothesis $H_1$: JaxMARL GPU throughput is significantly greater than CPU PettingZoo throughput for large batches ($B \\ge 1,024$).\n  - Significance test: Two-sided Welch's t-test / Mann-Whitney U test at $\\alpha = 0.01$.\n- **Crossover Point Analysis**: Determine the minimum batch size $B^*$ such that $S(B^*) > 1.0$ with $p < 0.01$.\n- **Amdahl & Scaling Saturation**: Fit throughput scaling curves to determine the asymptotic GPU saturation throughput ($\\lim_{B \\to \\infty} \\text{SPS}$) where memory bandwidth or GPU compute saturation caps parallel speedup.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility Assessment & Risk Mitigation\n\n1. **GPU Out-Of-Memory (OOM) at Scale**:\n   - *Risk*: At $B = 65,536$, storing large rollout buffers could exhaust GPU VRAM.\n   - *Mitigation*: MPE Simple Spread state representation per environment is small ($\\approx 200$ bytes). At $B = 65,536$, the active state array requires $< 20$ MB of VRAM. A rollout of $L = 1,000$ steps requires $< 2$ GB VRAM, well within an 80GB (or 24GB) GPU budget.\n2. **CPU Multiprocessing IPC Overhead**:\n   - *Risk*: Spawning thousands of parallel environments via Python `SubprocVectorEnv` incurs severe context-switching and IPC serialization overhead.\n   - *Mitigation*: Cap PettingZoo worker process count $C$ at the physical core limit (24 workers), batching sub-environments per worker, and avoid exceeding $B=2,048$ on CPU.\n3. **Execution Runtime Budget**:\n   - JaxMARL at high batch sizes ($B \\ge 4,096$) executes $1,000$ steps in under 1 second.\n   - CPU PettingZoo at $B=1$ takes $\\approx 1$ second for $1,000$ steps; at $B=128$ (24 cores) it takes $\\approx 10–20$ seconds.\n   - The total compute time for all parameter sweeps across 10 random seeds is estimated at $< 45$ minutes of host compute time, demonstrating high experimental feasibility.", "evidence": "Inspected workspace task.json, track.md, and system info. Received explicit researcher approval on the proposed experimental plan and methodology. Completed all components of the experimental design specification including timing synchronization, baseline configurations, independent/dependent variable matrices, controls, hardware/software stack, analysis/visualization plan, and feasibility assessment.", "assumptions": ["Task is design-only; no code execution or live benchmarking is performed in this workspace (per track.md).", "Hardware environment represents modern industry standard compute (e.g., NVIDIA A100 GPU and 24-core host CPU)